# Paths whose review is never automatic.
#
# GitHub requests a review from the owner on any pull request that touches
# these files. That request is the gate: everything NOT listed here can merge
# on green CI alone.
#
# The list is deliberately short. It is not "important files" — it is the set
# where CI cannot tell right from wrong, because the failure lands somewhere
# the test suite does not reach: real data, a real session, a real deployment.
#
# Rationale per entry is in CONTRIBUTING.md, "Which pull requests need a
# review".

# Data. `firestore.rules` is the only enforced access control in the product —
# the browser talks to Firestore directly, so a wrong rule is a live data leak
# that no unit test observes. There is also no backup or restore capability
# (#18), which makes every write-path change irreversible.
/firestore.rules            @ana-izaguirre
/src/lib/firebase.ts        @ana-izaguirre

# Shared contract. A change here reshapes records other screens already store.
/src/types.ts               @ana-izaguirre

# Deployment and the API key holder.
/server.ts                  @ana-izaguirre
/vercel.json                @ana-izaguirre

# Environment and CI. A workflow edit can disable the checks that guard
# everything else, so it cannot be guarded by those same checks.
/.github/                   @ana-izaguirre
/.env                       @ana-izaguirre
/.env.example               @ana-izaguirre

# Dependencies. A new package is new code from someone else, shipped to the
# browser, and no test in this repository reviews it.
/package.json               @ana-izaguirre
/package-lock.json          @ana-izaguirre
